body:
Benchmarked two versions of the same thing and the O(log n) one won. I've read the "cache locality" answer a hundred times but I want to know how much of it is that vs something else.

```cpp
// version A: ~340ms
std::unordered_map<int, int> m;
for (int k : keys) total += m[k];

// version B: ~120ms
std::sort(v.begin(), v.end());
for (int k : keys) total += std::lower_bound(v.begin(), v.end(), k)->second;
```

200k entries, 200k lookups, random int keys, `-O2`.

My guess is that every `unordered_map` lookup is a pointer chase into a node allocated somewhere unrelated, so it's a guaranteed cache miss, while the binary search touches maybe 4-5 cache lines and the top of the tree stays hot. But 3x seems like a lot.
